# DESCRIPTION

Unless said otherwise a call returns its result, or -1 with `errno` set to Linux's number for the error.
The file calls are fdio's: descriptors over the process's namespace, reached by Node and the fs protocol.
Paths are cleaned by name (`.` and `..` are resolved lexically), and are resolved against the current directory kept by the process.

## Descriptors

A descriptor names a file opened by `open` (see fcntl(2)): a channel to a node of the namespace, an offset and the flags it was opened with.
New descriptors are the lowest free number from 3, up to 1024 (then `EMFILE`).
Descriptors 0, 1 and 2, and any above them that the process was started with, are the rings of the process's standard I/O: they are read and written by number, never reused for a file, and cannot be duplicated (there is no `dup`).
Opening `/dev/fd/N` for such an N returns N itself.

`read` reads up to <count> bytes at the descriptor's offset and advances it.
It returns the number read, which is 0 at the end of the file and fewer than asked near it; a file that is not open for reading or a closed descriptor fails with `EBADF`, a directory with `EISDIR`.
Descriptor 0 reads standard input.
`pread` reads at <offset> and leaves the offset alone; a negative <offset> fails with `EINVAL`.
A <count> of 0 returns 0 without looking at the descriptor.

`write` writes <count> bytes at the descriptor's offset, or at the end of the file if it was opened with `O_APPEND`, and returns the number written, which is less than <count> only after a partial failure.
Descriptors 1 and 2 are the process's standard output and error: the write never fails, and standard output is gathered by line as stdio(2) says.
`pwrite` writes at <offset>.

`lseek` sets the offset to <offset> from the start (`SEEK_SET`), the current offset (`SEEK_CUR`) or the end of the file (`SEEK_END`), and returns the new offset.
A negative result, an overflow or another <whence> fails with `EINVAL`, as does `SEEK_END` on a directory.
The descriptors 0 to 2 cannot be seeked (`EBADF`).

`close` frees the descriptor; closing 0, 1 or 2 does nothing and returns 0.
Closing a descriptor the process was started with above 2 writes its end, if the process writes it.

`pipe` makes a pipe: one byte-stream ring of 8 KiB (D46, as a parent gives a child its descriptors), read as <fds>[0] and written as <fds>[1], two numbers no file holds, below 64.
A write sleeps while the ring is full and a read until some bytes come; once the write end is closed, a read returns what is left and then 0, and once the read end is closed, writes go nowhere and succeed.
It fails with `EMFILE` when there are no two free numbers, and `ENFILE` when the pipe can't be made (no memory, or 16 descriptors of this kind open already, those the process was started with among them).
`fsync` makes what was written durable; `ftruncate` sets the file's size, and fails with `EINVAL` if the file was not opened for writing or is a directory.
These two, like `O_TRUNC`, need the file's server to speak the fs protocol; one that does not answers with its own error.
`isatty` always returns 0 with `errno` set to `ENOTTY`.

## Names

`unlink` removes a file, `EISDIR` for a directory; `rmdir` removes an empty directory, `ENOTDIR` for a file and `ENOTEMPTY` for a directory with entries, and `EBUSY` for `/`.
`access` succeeds if the path exists, whatever the <mode> (a <mode> outside `F_OK`, `R_OK`, `W_OK` and `X_OK` is `EINVAL`): there are no permissions.
`getcwd` writes the current directory into <buffer> of <size> bytes and returns it; it fails with `ERANGE` if it does not fit and `EINVAL` if <size> is 0.
With a null <buffer> it returns memory from `malloc`, whatever <size>.
The directory is `/` at first.
`chdir` makes a directory the current one; it fails with `ENOTDIR` for a file.

The namespace has no links.
`readlink` fails with `EINVAL` for a path that exists, since it is not a link, and `ENOENT` for one that does not; `symlink` and `link` fail with `EPERM`.

## The system

`sysconf` answers `_SC_PAGESIZE` and `_SC_PAGE_SIZE` (4096), `_SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF` and `_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN` (the processors croi runs, all online), and `_SC_PHYS_PAGES` (the pages of RAM croi manages, from the vDSO's topology page); for any other name it returns -1 with `errno` `EINVAL`.
`getpagesize` returns 4096.
`gethostname` writes `localhost` and a NUL into <name>; it fails with `ENAMETOOLONG` if <size> is 9 or less.
`getpid` returns the low 32 bits of the process's koid, which croi never reuses (1 if the kernel cannot say).
`getentropy` fills <buffer> with <length> bytes, at most 256, from croi's random number generator; more fails with `EINVAL`.
`arc4random_buf` fills it from the same generator, any length, and can't fail; Embedded Swift's hasher seeds itself from it.

